        "self_reported": {
          "assumptions": [
            "Copying happens whether or not stdout is a terminal (the brief's flagged judgment call): the key is an explicit per-machine opt-in, so piped and --json runs copy too.",
            "The clipboard text ends in one trailing newline for every block (the opener's lines joined with LF plus a final LF; sentinel blocks as printed), matching what the pre-session probe tee carried.",
            "`clipboard_command = \"\"` at the global layer reads as unset, as C's accessor already did."
          ],
          "judgment_calls": [
            "The probe asks bale through a new verb, `bale clipboard` (stdin in, exit 0 copied / 1 not copied / other = verb unavailable), rather than a print-the-command flag: bale runs the copy, so the probe and every other paste point share one runner and one notice.",
            "A request-shipped project key still bakes into the scaffold, but only as clip_fallback for a machine without a usable bale; when bale answers (0 or 1) the script never second-guesses it.",
            "No 'run bale config init' nudge on pack/relay/apply when unset: those commands stay silent for operators who never opted in. The nudge lives in `bale clipboard` (the probe path), the status row, and the probe's runtime remedy.",
            "The copy helper lives in bale_report.py (in forecast, beside the stream-discipline helpers) rather than a new bin/ sibling, which would have pulled scripts/build.sh and the upgrade member list out of forecast.",
            "Status row label is `probe clipboard`, matching the rider's wording and the key's section until the neutral rename.",
            "The accessor's fail() line is captured (not printed as `[bale] error:`) and restated in the notice; fail() still journals it into an open session log.",
            "Clipboard commands are bounded at 10s; a stuck one is stopped and reported.",
            "validation.sh runs the touched suites as two parallel groups (~74s observed) and gates the full discover behind --slow (~7 min)."
          ],
